The only encoding that I'm really familiar with (having
implemented it) is BER.  It contains a lot of features not
really necessary for a file which is only to be read by the same
program (not necessarily the same binary) which wrote it.

> But in what respect do you think it's heavy? Especially
> compared to XML its representation is extremely compact,
> whatever encoding you choose, even if you consider BER.

Compared to XML, yes.  Compared to just about any other format
I'm aware of, on the other hand...  My first reaction would be
to start with XDR, and add meta-information as needed.

> The encoders/decoders, OTOH, are indeed "heavy" (i.e. complex).

Yes and no.  If you're using floating point, and concerned about
interfacing between two machines with different floating point
representations, it's incredibly complex.  If you avoid floating
point and variable length integers, and use only a small number
of user defined types, it doesn't have to be that difficult.

> I've no numbers how "heavy" they are compared to XML parsers.
> XML's advantage is that it's quite well supported by languages
> and libraries, and it's popular and wide-spread known which
> makes it a natural candidate.

The fact that it's widespread, and that libraries already exist
for parsing it, is definitely the main advantage of XML.  But I
don't think we'd need something anywhere near that complex, and
a custom written parser for XDR with annotations shouldn't be
much more work than integrating an existing XML parser.

